WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.537
In this lesson you'll learn a five-step habit

2
00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:07.777
for getting unstuck on anything technical,

3
00:00:07.777 --> 00:00:10.273
and how AI changed who does each step

4
00:00:10.273 --> 00:00:13.061
without changing why the steps matter.

5
00:00:13.663 --> 00:00:16.879
Read the error, search its exact words,

6
00:00:16.879 --> 00:00:18.620
check the official docs,

7
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.221
ask the AI with the evidence on its desk,

8
00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:23.833
and settle it with a tiny test:

9
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:26.887
AI can now do most of the legwork, so your job

10
00:00:26.887 --> 00:00:29.545
is to give it the clues and check the verdict.

11
00:00:29.545 --> 00:00:32.007
Every district ahead brings new errors;

12
00:00:32.007 --> 00:00:34.226
this works on all of them.

13
00:00:34.676 --> 00:00:36.986
This lesson is the last stop at the Start,

14
00:00:36.986 --> 00:00:38.333
before The Machine.

15
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:40.771
Lesson one said this is learned through forums

16
00:00:40.771 --> 00:00:44.314
and breaking things; here it becomes a habit.

17
00:00:45.079 --> 00:00:47.888
Five years ago: read the traceback,

18
00:00:47.888 --> 00:00:50.767
Google it, land on Stack Overflow,

19
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:53.020
check the docs, try something.

20
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:55.794
Stack Overflow was the wall of case reports:

21
00:00:55.794 --> 00:00:58.256
over a million new questions a year.

22
00:00:58.256 --> 00:01:00.392
Today, new questions are down

23
00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.633
about ninety-nine percent from peak.

24
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:04.060
It was already shrinking;

25
00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.824
after ChatGPT, it fell off a cliff.

26
00:01:06.824 --> 00:01:08.414
The old answers are still there,

27
00:01:08.414 --> 00:01:10.506
and inside the AI.

28
00:01:10.956 --> 00:01:13.615
Getting unstuck is solving a small case.

29
00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:15.600
One case file with five tabs:

30
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.698
clue, search, rulebook, briefing, experiment.

31
00:01:19.698 --> 00:01:22.763
In twenty twenty-one, you worked every tab alone.

32
00:01:22.763 --> 00:01:25.562
Now last lesson's clerk sits beside you,

33
00:01:25.562 --> 00:01:27.802
knowing only what's on its desk.

34
00:01:27.802 --> 00:01:30.693
In a chat app, you carry the evidence to it;

35
00:01:30.693 --> 00:01:33.155
an agent walks to your files itself.

36
00:01:33.155 --> 00:01:36.034
Either way, you sign the last page.

37
00:01:36.034 --> 00:01:37.973
Here the picture breaks.

38
00:01:37.973 --> 00:01:39.691
An error isn't a culprit;

39
00:01:39.691 --> 00:01:41.757
it's a note written to help you.

40
00:01:41.757 --> 00:01:43.441
There's no final reveal,

41
00:01:43.441 --> 00:01:45.811
just the next step that works.

42
00:01:46.261 --> 00:01:49.140
First tab: the clue.

43
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.973
An error names the file, the line,

44
00:01:51.973 --> 00:01:53.657
and the kind of failure.

45
00:01:53.657 --> 00:01:55.979
Find the line that names the failure;

46
00:01:55.979 --> 00:01:59.566
in a Python traceback, that's the last one.

47
00:01:59.566 --> 00:02:01.632
Errors got friendlier too;

48
00:02:01.632 --> 00:02:04.964
many suggest the fix: did you mean...

49
00:02:04.964 --> 00:02:07.194
Agents read errors too;

50
00:02:07.194 --> 00:02:10.050
the error is your check they understood.

51
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.755
Second tab: search the exact message,

52
00:02:12.755 --> 00:02:15.506
minus your own names and paths.

53
00:02:15.506 --> 00:02:18.397
The tool's words find everyone who hit it.

54
00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:20.417
Search an error today and you usually

55
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:23.946
get an AI answer first, sources underneath.

56
00:02:23.946 --> 00:02:25.991
Click through to the source.

57
00:02:26.442 --> 00:02:28.694
Third tab: the rulebook,

58
00:02:28.694 --> 00:02:30.819
the official docs for your version.

59
00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:32.363
Everything else is a summary;

60
00:02:32.363 --> 00:02:34.151
summaries go stale.

61
00:02:34.151 --> 00:02:40.374
Docs now have a second door for AI: llms.txt,

62
00:02:40.374 --> 00:02:44.089
a simple file many docs publish for AI tools.

63
00:02:44.089 --> 00:02:47.688
Microsoft, Amazon and Google also run servers

64
00:02:47.688 --> 00:02:50.358
so AI can search their current docs.

65
00:02:50.358 --> 00:02:53.028
Make sure the AI read your version's rulebook,

66
00:02:53.028 --> 00:02:54.387
not its memory.

67
00:02:54.387 --> 00:02:56.488
Fourth tab: the briefing.

68
00:02:56.488 --> 00:02:58.869
The exact error, your versions,

69
00:02:58.869 --> 00:03:02.328
the command, the file, on the clerk's desk.

70
00:03:02.328 --> 00:03:04.813
Agents gather most of it themselves.

71
00:03:04.813 --> 00:03:07.994
Ask for the explanation, not just the fix.

72
00:03:07.994 --> 00:03:10.119
In one study this January, learners

73
00:03:10.119 --> 00:03:13.439
who let AI write the code understood less,

74
00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:15.204
especially at debugging;

75
00:03:15.204 --> 00:03:17.748
those who asked it to explain did better.

76
00:03:18.211 --> 00:03:21.312
Most assistants now offer a learning mode.

77
00:03:21.762 --> 00:03:25.338
The new risk: the clerk as a confident witness,

78
00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:27.486
fluent and almost right.

79
00:03:27.486 --> 00:03:30.574
In Stack Overflow's twenty twenty-five survey,

80
00:03:30.574 --> 00:03:32.466
almost-right answers topped

81
00:03:32.466 --> 00:03:36.054
developers' frustrations, picked by two-thirds.

82
00:03:36.054 --> 00:03:38.132
Only about three in a hundred highly

83
00:03:38.132 --> 00:03:41.255
trust AI answers, yet most use them.

84
00:03:41.255 --> 00:03:43.009
It goes wrong three ways.

85
00:03:43.009 --> 00:03:45.272
Functions since renamed or retired;

86
00:03:45.272 --> 00:03:47.618
models learned from older code.

87
00:03:47.618 --> 00:03:50.555
Command flags that sound right and don't exist.

88
00:03:50.555 --> 00:03:52.749
And packages that don't exist:

89
00:03:52.749 --> 00:03:54.897
in one twenty twenty-six study,

90
00:03:54.897 --> 00:03:58.624
about one AI coding answer in twenty named one.

91
00:03:58.624 --> 00:04:00.551
Attackers register those names,

92
00:04:00.551 --> 00:04:03.663
so the install works, with their code inside.

93
00:04:03.663 --> 00:04:05.509
The picture breaks here too:

94
00:04:05.509 --> 00:04:07.331
this witness saw nothing.

95
00:04:07.331 --> 00:04:10.477
It predicts, so it can be fluent and wrong.

96
00:04:10.477 --> 00:04:13.567
Checking matters more now, not less.

97
00:04:14.018 --> 00:04:17.594
The last tab settles it: a tiny test.

98
00:04:17.594 --> 00:04:19.347
The smallest run that proves

99
00:04:19.347 --> 00:04:22.493
or disproves an answer, in a scratch file.

100
00:04:22.493 --> 00:04:25.373
Pass or fail, you know more.

101
00:04:25.373 --> 00:04:27.927
Coding agents like Claude Code and Codex

102
00:04:27.927 --> 00:04:30.980
are built around this, and can run it for you.

103
00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:33.581
You still need to know what it proves.

104
00:04:33.581 --> 00:04:36.460
The direction: agents doing more of the loop,

105
00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:39.084
and more docs written for machines first.

106
00:04:39.084 --> 00:04:42.996
Your job: clues in, verdict checked.

107
00:04:42.996 --> 00:04:45.005
Safe to ignore for now:

108
00:04:45.005 --> 00:04:48.117
memorising flags, which tool is best,

109
00:04:48.117 --> 00:04:50.173
and forum etiquette.

110
00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.225
The error is the first clue,

111
00:04:52.225 --> 00:04:54.791
and its exact words are the search key.

112
00:04:54.791 --> 00:04:56.904
Find the line that names the failure,

113
00:04:56.904 --> 00:04:58.704
and never paraphrase it.

114
00:04:58.704 --> 00:05:00.747
Give the AI the evidence, then check

115
00:05:00.747 --> 00:05:03.406
its verdict against the docs for your version.

116
00:05:03.406 --> 00:05:05.275
A tiny test settles it.

117
00:05:05.275 --> 00:05:08.282
Until a run proves it, an answer is a guess.

118
00:05:08.282 --> 00:05:11.452
AI is the lantern: it lights every district,

119
00:05:11.452 --> 00:05:14.286
but it doesn't walk the streets for you.
